VERSION = "2026-11-17:1359"

# RELEASE_VERSION is the human-facing SemVer identifier (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH,
# optionally with a -rc.N/-beta.N/-alpha.N pre-release suffix) shown to users
# alongside the VERSION build stamp above. It's purely a display label —
# release tagging and the update-available check both key off VERSION (the
# build timestamp), not this, so bumping it doesn't affect either. The
# -rc.N/-beta.N/-alpha.N counter advances only when an actual release is cut
# (decided 2026-09-21 -- previously scripts/bump_version.py auto-incremented
# it on every dev-session bump, which drifted it away from the real release
# count); bump the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH part by hand only for a deliberate
# milestone (e.g. reaching 1.0.0).
RELEASE_VERSION = "0.1.0-rc.29"

# A short, plain-language note shown next to VERSION on the home page and
# used to describe this build in the update-available banner (see
# numa_app/services/update_check.py). Hand-updated at the same time as
# VERSION, alongside the changelog entry it summarizes — pick
# whichever of these best matches that entry's size, or write a short one
# of your own in the same spirit. Don't point readers at "the manual" or
# "the top of the manual" here — home.html already appends its own link to
# the manual's What's new section right after this note, so the note itself only
# needs to describe the change, not say where to read more:
#   "minor problem fixes"
#   "minor function added or improved"
#   "significant improvements implemented"
# Once the note grows past a sentence or two, write it in two parts, kept on
# one line (no newlines: it is copied into the manual's one-line "Version
# overview:"): "SUMMARY: <one succinct sentence> DETAILS: <the full note>".
# The labels are in capitals. Each time the details change, re-check the
# summary against them: every claim must still be true and must not
# overstate the details.
NEW_VERSION_NOTE = (
    "SUMMARY: Each food's data problems, and their fixes, are now in one place; smarter searches; checkboxes for \"complete\" added to lists and searches, and for \"drinks\" in the Recipes list and in searches; Compare keeps your picks across searches; a shorter, reorganized manual with a Return button; recipe Edit can report what each ingredient change does to the recipe's nutrients; filling in a food's missing data has clear steps, ending with Claude AI; What-if removes a recipe logged as individual ingredients even after you changed it for that meal; Alt+Shift+K lists every page and shortcut. "
    "DETAILS: New Problems by food page (Foods → 9) and an Everything to check on this food box on each food's page: all of a food's data problems in one place, with their fixes. Searches no longer put partial matches from your own data above full matches when you already have an item matching every word, singular and plural search words match each other, a food can be deleted from its own page, and each meal item now records and shows the time of day it was entered. Recipes can be marked complete or as drinks, and foods as drinks, straight from the Recipes list and every search; arrow keys move through search results, and Back returns to the one you opened; Print pages and other NuMa links stay in NuMa's own window; quiet buttons are blue; the manual's search opens the section you picked, with your words highlighted; and Compare ticks are kept as you search again or move between lists, so one comparison can gather foods from several searches. A `!` before a search word leaves out results with that word (`beans red !kidney cooked`), and the list says how many results they hid; a search for a cooked food no longer misses canned products online; a hint under every search box sums up how to write a search; and NuMa offers to search USDA again with your words reversed, which finds many branded products. On the Annotate page, each estimate being asked for has its own Skip button beside it, and the Meals & Log list's Complete column is a checkbox, so a meal can be marked complete without opening it. On a meal, the Add Food or Recipe header, search box and search hint line up along the top, and the hint's Learn more opens the manual at word order. A `!` word works in list filter boxes too (Recipes, Food Cache, My Pantry, Search meal history and the rest). Removing an item from a meal no longer makes the page jump around. The User Manual is reorganized and much shorter, with step-by-step workflows. A Return button above the manual (Alt+Shift+U) goes straight back to the page you came from, and Manual reopens it where you left off; and Settings checks a USDA API key with USDA before saving it, with a Test key button. The Daily Summary page now says it covers several days and drops its date box and Today's summary button; a day opens from its date in the table. Its buttons now say what they show: Nutrient averages over a period (the last 7, 14 or 30 days, any dates, or chosen meals; this replaces the separate Nutrient totals button, which opened the same page) and Week-by-week averages (was Blocks of days); the date-range buttons read Average these dates and Average these meals, and each block's All nutrients link (was Full nutrient totals). On a recipe's Edit page, adding, removing or changing the amount of an ingredient offers a short report on how the recipe's nutrients per serving moved since the last report (nutrient groups you pick, all nutrients, or only those that changed by 5% or more), written to recipe-edit-effects-data.md and shown by a button above Add Ingredient; food and recipe names there open in a panel over the page, so a look-up doesn't leave it. The Recipes list's filter now keeps only recipes whose names hold every word you type, like the other list filters (it used to keep any recipe matching any one word, so `coffee 2` listed every recipe with a 2 in its name). Edit Recipe has a Contents list down the side (Recipe details and its parts, Add Ingredient, Ingredients) that jumps to each part and opens it if collapsed, and a Clear search button right after Search; its search results show each recipe's DCP per serving and description, right after Code. A food's Edit page ends with an Ask Claude AI for help box, which writes a prompt for one nutrient group or the whole profile, asking only for the values the food is missing or for every value to replace doubtful ones; the No amino acid data box on a food's page lists its three fixes in order, ending with a Write a prompt for Claude AI button; and the manual's new Workflow 12 gives the order to try: a better-documented record of the same food, then a similar food, then Claude AI. A recipe added to a meal as individual ingredients now marks each of those foods with the recipe (shown as part of on the meal page), so What-if: meals can remove it or change its amount even after it was changed for that meal (an ingredient left out, an amount changed, a food swapped); foods added by hand stay, and recipes logged that way earlier are still found by matching their ingredients. Where a change can't reach such foods, a Replace of the recipe or a sub-recipe inside a recipe logged that way, What-if and its Apply page name the meals to fix by hand. Alt+Shift+K (or Ctrl+K) opens a command palette: every menu page and this page's own shortcuts, each with its keys, filtered as you type, plus searches for the typed text and Quit; Settings' Keyboard Shortcuts section moves to Alt+Shift+E. A page that reloads after a change below its search results keeps your place even when the online results arrive late."
)
